MAYOR OF PALANGKA RAYA,

Considering a. that every citizen has the right to survival, growth, and proper development and to obtain a sense of security that is consistent and systematic as a form of Protection for Women and Children as mandated in the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;

b. that the Regional Government, families, groups, social organizations and/or other community organizations have responsibility for the implementation of fulfillment, protection, respect, advancement and enforcement of rights in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;

c. that to provide direction, legal basis and legal certainty in the Protection of Women and Children, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation on the Protection of Women and Children;

d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation on the Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence;

Bearing in mind:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;

2. Law Number 5 of 1965 concerning the Establishment of Palangka Raya Municipality (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1965 Number 48, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 2753);

3. Law Number 7 of 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (Convention on the Elimination of all Form of Discrimination Against Woman) (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3277);

4. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);

5. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235) as last amended by Law Number 17 of 2016 concerning Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 1 of 2016 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection into Law (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2016 Number 99, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5882);

6. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);

7. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning the Eradication of the Crime of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);

8. Law Number 52 of 2009 concerning Population Development and Family Development (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2009 Number 161, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5080);

9. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);

10. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning the Eradication of the Crime of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);

11. Government Regulation Number 59 of 2019 concerning Implementation of Child Protection Coordination (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2019 Number 160, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6382);

12. Regulation of the Minister of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 4 of 2018 concerning Guidelines for the Establishment of Regional Technical Implementation Units for the Protection of Women and Children (State News of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2018 Number 532);

13. Regional Regulation of Palangka Raya City Number 17 of 2013 concerning Child Protection in the Framework of Realizing a Child-Friendly City (Regional Gazette of Palangka Raya City Year 2017 Number 17, Supplement to the Regional Gazette Number 8);

With the Mutual Approval of THE REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF PALANGKA RAYA CITY and

Article 3

Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence shall be implemented based on the principles of: a. responsive to rights;
b. non-discrimination;
c. maintaining confidentiality;
d. empowerment; and e. participation.

Article 4

The objectives of Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence include: a. ensuring the fulfillment of rights;
b. providing justice and equality of rights;
c. providing protection and security; and d. conducting empowerment.

Article 5

The scope of Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence includes: a. protection of Women;

b. protection of Children;
c. coordination and cooperation;
d. responsibility of Regional Government;
e. community participation;
f. guidance and supervision;
g. evaluation;

h. reporting; and

i. funding.

Article 6

Protection of Women includes:
a. services;
b. empowerment;
c. prevention; and d. monitoring.

Article 7

(1) Forms of services for Protection of Women as referred to in Article 6 letter a include: a. legal aid;
b. health services;
c. social rehabilitation services;

d. complaint, consultation and counseling services;
e. social reintegration services; and
f. pre-marital guidance services.

(2) Pre-marital guidance services as referred to in paragraph (1) letter f, shall be implemented by every woman who will enter into marriage.

(3) Guidance conducted as referred to in paragraph (2) is to provide understanding and knowledge regarding family resilience, health, psychology, religion, social and culture.
(4) Guidance as referred to in paragraph (3) shall be implemented by the Regional Apparatus that administers affairs in the field of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection.
(5) In implementing guidance as referred to in paragraph (4) the Regional Apparatus may involve: a. related agencies;
b. religious leaders; and c. customary leaders.
(6) Further provisions regarding minimum service standards for the protection of Women shall be regulated by Mayor Regulation.

Article 11

Protection of Children includes: a. services;
b. fulfillment of Children's rights;
c. prevention; and d. monitoring.

Article 13

1. Fulfillment of Children's Rights as referred to in Article 11 letter b includes: a. guarantee of civil rights and freedoms;

b. guarantee of family environment and alternative care;
c. guarantee of basic health and welfare; d. education, leisure time, culture and recreation; and e. special protection.
2. Special protection as referred to in paragraph (1) letter e, shall be conducted for: a. children in emergency situations;
b. children in conflict with the law;
c. children from minority and isolated groups;
d. children who are exploited economically and/or sexually;
e. children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic and other addictive substance abuse;
f. children who are victims of pornography;
g. children with HIV/AIDS;
h. children victims of kidnapping, sale and/or trafficking;
i. children victims of physical and/or psychological violence;
j. children victims of sexual crimes;
k. children victims of terrorism networks;

l. children with disabilities;

m. children victims of maltreatment and neglect;
n. children with deviant social behavior; and o. children who are victims of stigmatization from labeling related to their parents' condition.
(3) Fulfillment of Children's Rights as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented in order to realize a Child-Friendly City.
4. Fulfillment of Children's Rights as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be conducted in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations.

Article 14

(1) Prevention of violations of Children's rights shall be implemented by the Regional Government and the community.
(2) Prevention of violations of Children's rights implemented by the Regional Government as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented by: a. establishing a network;
b. conducting coordination, integration, synchronization of prevention based on partnership patterns with the community, private sector, non-governmental organizations; and c. conducting socialization of laws and regulations related to Protection of Children.
3. Prevention of violations of Children's rights implemented by the community as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented by: a. increasing Children's productivity;

b. developing Children's self-potential;
c. preventing child marriage;
d. reducing maternal and infant mortality rates; and e. preventing the occurrence of domestic violence.
4. Establishment of a network as referred to in paragraph (2) letter a, includes work partners of the Regional Government in the implementation of prevention of rights violations in the form of family partners.

(5) Further provisions regarding prevention of violations of Children's rights as referred to in paragraph (3), shall be regulated in Mayor Regulation.

CHAPTER IV

COORDINATION AND COOPERATION

Article 16

1. The implementation of coordination and cooperation for the Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence aims to:
a. improve efforts to fulfill the rights of Women and Children;
b. improve synergistic and harmonious working relationships; and
c. obtain data and information in the implementation of the Protection of Women and Children.
2. In order to achieve the objectives as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government cooperates with:
a. other Regional Governments;
b. related agencies;
c. non-governmental institutions;
d. universities; and
e. non-governmental organizations.
3. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (2) includes:
a. exchange of data and information;
b. socialization or legal counseling lectures;
c. rehabilitation of victims of violence; and
d. repatriation/returning and social reintegration.

Article 17

(1) The Regional Government is responsible for the protection of the rights of Women and Children.

(2) The responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. education;
b. health;
c. politics;
d. law;
e. economy;
f. culture; and
g. social.
3. The responsibilities of the Regional Government for the protection of the rights of Women as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out by:
a. formulating Regional policies that are gender responsive;
b. institutionalizing gender mainstreaming within the Regional Government;
c. empowering Women in the fields of politics, law, economy, culture and social affairs in community organizations at the city level;
d. strengthening and developing service provider institutions for Women's empowerment at the city level;
e. implementing efforts to prevent violence against Women.
4. The responsibilities of the Regional Government for the protection of the rights of Children as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out by:
a. formulating policies, programs, and Regional development activities that are child rights-oriented which are set forth in Regional development planning documents;
b. formulating norms, standards, procedures and criteria for the assessment of the fulfillment and protection of Children's rights which refer to the norms, standards, procedures and criteria established by the government;
c. implementing child-friendly cities in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
d. facilitating the implementation of Regional Children's forums;
e. increasing community participation in the implementation and protection of Children's rights;
f. monitoring and evaluation of the implementation of programs for the fulfillment and Protection of Children;
g. supervising Child Protection institutions;
h. providing special protection for Children; and
i. implementing efforts to prevent violence against Children.

Article 21

(J.) The technical service institution as referred to in Article 20 paragraph (2) letter a is implemented by P2TP2A and FPK2PA.

2. In the implementation of the Protection of Women and Children, it is carried out by the Regional Technical Implementation Unit.
(3) In the case of implementing technical services as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government may establish a task force.

(4) The task force as referred to in paragraph (3) may also carry out institutional strengthening through cooperation and coordination with:
a. KLA task force;
b. KRA task force;
c. schools and/or educational institutions;
d. Children's forum;
e. children's center;
f. community organizations;
g. Women's organizations; and
h. other institutions that care about the fulfillment of rights and the Protection of Children.
5. The task force as referred to in paragraph (4) is a coordinative institution at the city, sub-district and urban village levels that coordinates policies, programs and activities to realize sub-districts and Child-Friendly urban villages.
6. Further provisions regarding technical services as referred to in paragraph (3) are regulated by Mayor Regulation.

Article 25

The forms of services to victims organized by P2TP2A include:
a. medical services;
b. medicolegal services;
c. psychosocial services;
d. legal services;
e. social reintegration services; and
f. economic independence services.

Article 28

Parents are obliged and responsible to:  a. care for, nurture, educate, manage and protect family members;
b. develop family members in accordance with their abilities, talents, and interests; and  c. prevent marriage at the age of Children.

Article 29

Increasing the role of families in the Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence is carried out through:  a. prevention of marriage at the age of Children;
b. bringing Women closer to health services; and c. strengthening family resilience.

